  "id": "901742290",
  "subject": [],
  "title": "Villa of Agrippa Postumus",
  "provenance": "Pleiades",
  "placeTypeURIs": [
    "https://pleiades.stoa.org/vocabularies/place-types/villa"
  ],
  "details": "<p>The scholarly consensus holds that the villa was built between 21 and 16 BCE and that the property passed to Agrippa Postumus in 11 BCE based on an inscribed roof tile. The question of who was responsible for the villa's management and who oversaw its decoration remains debated, with some scholars wanting to see Iulia Maior as having had this responsibility. The villa's final owner was one\u00a0<span>Euthyeus, a freedman of Claudius.</span></p>\r\n<p><span>As Della Corte only excavated a portion of the villa, our knowledge is limited. The recovered wall paintings that are now divided among museums in Naples and New York come from four rooms in the villa's southern corridor. The paintings belong to Mau's Third Style of Pompeian Wall painting and are usually adjudged as among the finest specimens of wall painting from the Augustan regime. The strong influence of Isiac and Egyptianizing motifs is to be noted.</span></p>",
  "review_state": "published",
  "type": "FeatureCollection",
  "description": "The Villa of Agrippa Postumus was discovered at Boscotrecase in 1903 on land owned by Cavaliere Ernesto Santini. Works for the Circumvesuviana railroad line brought the site to light. Excavations supervised by Matteo Della Corte were carried out from 1903 to 1905. In 1906, however, a new eruption of Mount Vesuvius reburied the site. Della Corte published a plan of the excavated portion of the villa in the Notizie degli scavi di antichit\u00e0.",
